A race needs two threads to collide in a narrow window. Starting them one after another almost never hits it — you have to release them together and repeat.

<pre><code><span class="c">// 50 threads, one seat: the invariant is that exactly one succeeds</span>
<span class="k">var</span> start = <span class="k">new</span> CountDownLatch(<span class="n">1</span>);
<span class="k">var</span> ok = <span class="k">new</span> AtomicInteger();
<span class="k">try</span> (<span class="k">var</span> pool = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    IntStream.range(<span class="n">0</span>, <span class="n">50</span>).forEach(i -&gt; pool.submit(() -&gt; {
        start.await();                       <span class="c">// all threads wait on the same gate</span>
        <span class="k">try</span> { booking.hold(seatId); ok.incrementAndGet(); }
        <span class="k">catch</span> (SeatTaken | OptimisticLockException e) { <span class="c">/* expected loser */</span> }
        <span class="k">return null</span>;
    }));
    start.countDown();                       <span class="c">// released together</span>
}
assertThat(ok.get()).isEqualTo(<span class="n">1</span>);
assertThat(seats.find(seatId).version()).isEqualTo(<span class="n">1</span>);</code></pre>

**What makes such a test worth having:**

- assert an [[g:invariant]], not a schedule: exactly one winner, stock never negative, no duplicate charge — not "thread A ran first"
- run it against the real database, because the guarantee comes from the unique index or the row lock, not from Java (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2)
- repeat it; a single pass on a quiet machine means nothing

:::warn Green means "not observed", never "impossible"
An interleaving that needs a 20-microsecond window may appear once in ten thousand runs, and only on a loaded CI machine. Concurrency tests find bugs; they do not prove their absence.
:::

:::deep
- reproduce first, then fix. A [[b:failing test that fails only sometimes]] is still evidence; add a repeat count and run it under load until the failure rate is stable enough to work with.
- correctness must come from a mechanism you can point at: a unique constraint, `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`, an optimistic `@Version`, or a single-writer partition. If the answer is "we tested it a lot", there is no mechanism.
- for low-level memory-model questions (visibility, reordering) hand-rolled tests are close to useless — [[o:JCStress]] exists precisely because the JVM is allowed to do things your test will never observe.
- instrument the loser path too: silently swallowing `OptimisticLockException` in a test is how a retry storm ships to production unnoticed (25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q5).
- deadlock tests belong here as well: two transactions taking the same rows in opposite orders, asserting that one is aborted rather than both hanging (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q4).
:::
